    transliteration: "Maṇ tiṇinta nilaṉum,\nNilaṉ ēntiya vicumpum,\nVicumpu taivaru vaḷiyum,\nVaḷit talaiiya tīyum,\nTī muraṇiya nīrum eṉṟāṅku\nAimperum pūtattu iyaṟkai pōlap,\nPōṟṟārp poṟuttalum, cūḻcciyatu akalamum,\nValiyum, teṟalum, aḷiyoṭu puṇarnta\nKāṭciyum, āṟṟaleṉṟa aintum,\nUṭaiyōy nīyē; avai, uṭaiyōrkku…",
    english: "The earth set firm with soil,\nthe sky that lifts and bears the earth,\nthe wind that strokes the sky,\nthe fire that the wind kindles,\nthe water that opposes the fire — thus,\nlike the natures of these five great elements,\nyou alone possess all five qualities:\nendurance even of those who do not honour you,\nbreadth of counsel,\nstrength,\npower to destroy,\nand vision joined with grace.\nThese five belong to you, O lord!\nGood is the rest that comes\nto those who win the rare prize of your raised arms!",
    tamilUrai: "சேரமான் உதியன் சேரலாதனை வாழ்த்தும் இப்பாடல், ஐம்பெரும் பூதங்களின் இயற்கையோடு மன்னனின் ஐந்து குணங்களை ஒப்பிடுகிறது — பகைவரையும் பொறுத்தல், ஆராய்ச்சியின் அகலம், வலிமை, அழிக்கும் ஆற்றல், அருளுடன் கூடிய காட்சி. \"பெருஞ்சோற்று\" உதியன் என்ற பெயரே, பாரதப் போரில் இரு படைகளுக்கும் சோறிட்டான் என்ற புராண மரபை நினைவுறுத்துகிறது.",
    altTranslation: "Like the firm earth, the sky that bears it,\nthe wind that brushes the sky, the fire wind kindles,\nthe water that resists the fire — \nyou too combine the natures of all five great elements:\npatience with foes, breadth of counsel,\nmight, the power to destroy,\nand sight made gentle by grace.\nThese five are yours, great lord —\nblessed are they who find their rest beneath your raised, sustaining arms.",
    note: "Composed by Murañciyūr Muṭināgarāyar in praise of the Cēra king Utiyaṉ Cēralātaṉ — the king famous in tradition as 'Peruñcōṟṟu Utiyaṉ' (Utiyaṉ of the great feast), said to have fed both armies during the Mahabharata war. The poem's structure — five elements paralleling five royal virtues — is a classic Sangam panegyric device."
